# Date Handling (UTC)

EventBook treats all dates as **UTC**. Format and serialize dates only through helpers in `@/lib/format-date` — not loose `new Date().toISOString()` or `toLocaleDateString()` in pages, components, or API handlers.

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
import { formatDate } from "@/lib/format-date";
const label = formatDate(eventDate, "date"); // display
const stored = formatDate(eventDate, "iso"); // persistence / JSON

// ❌ BAD
eventDate.toISOString();
new Date().toISOString();
date.toLocaleDateString("pl-PL");
```

## Rules

- Import `formatDate` (and any parse helpers) from `@/lib/format-date` only — do not duplicate date logic inline.
- Persist and transmit timestamps as UTC ISO strings produced via `formatDate(..., "iso")` or the project's parse helper — never mix local timezone offsets in stored values.
- Display strings for UI (lists, forms, emails) use `formatDate(..., "date" | "datetime")` — not raw ISO in user-facing copy.
- Accept user input as strings; parse through `@/lib/format-date` before comparing or storing — do not rely on implicit local `Date` parsing in feature code.

## API & database

- JSON fields and Supabase columns: UTC ISO strings only.
- Do not embed formatted locale strings in API responses; send ISO UTC and format on the client/server at render time with `formatDate()`.

## Tests

Colocate tests in `src/lib/format-date.test.ts` for new format modes or edge cases (invalid input, midnight UTC).

Canonical implementation: @src/lib/format-date.ts

See @AGENTS.md for repo-wide rules.
